pressions, HCF x LCM equals</h4>\n<p>A) HCF + LCM</p>\n<p>B) the larger expression</p>\n<p>C) product of the two expressions</p>\n<p>UNIT 6: Linear Equation and Inequalities</p>\n<p>11. Solve 3x + 7 = 22.</p>\n<p>A) x=3</p>\n<p>B) x=5</p>\n<p>C) x=7</p>\n<p>D) x=15</p>\n<h4>12. Solving -2x &lt; 6 gives</h4>\n<p>B) x &gt; -3</p>\n<p>C) x &lt; 3</p>\n<p>D) x &gt; 3</p>\n<p>UNIT 7: Linear Graphs and their Applications</p>\n<h4>13. The graph of x = 3 is</h4>\n<p>A) a horizontal line</p>\n<p>B) a vertical line</p>\n<p>C) a line through origin with slope 3</p>\n<p>D) the x-axis</p>\n<h4>14. Point (2,-5) lies in quadrant</h4>\n<h4>A) I</h4>\n<p>B) II</p>\n<p>C) III</p>\n<p>D) IV</p>\n<p>UNIT 8: Quadratic Equations</p>\n<p>15. Solve x^2 - 9 = 0.</p>\n<p>A) x=3 only</p>\n<p>B) x=-3 only</p>\n<p>C) x=3 or x=-3</p>\n<p>D) x=9 or x=-9</p>\n<p>16. Discriminant of x^2 + 6x + 9 = 0 is:</p>\n<h4>A) -36</h4>\n<h4>B) 0</h4>\n<h4>C) 9</h4>\n<h4>D) 36</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="4">\n<h2>Page 4</h2>\n<p>UNIT 9: Congruent Triangles</p>\n<p>17. Which condition proves congruence of right triangles using hypotenuse and one leg?</p>\n<p>A) SSS</p>\n<p>B) ASA</p>\n<p>C) RHS</p>\n<p>D) AAA</p>\n<h4>18. AAA for two triangles shows</h4>\n<p>A) congruence always</p>\n<p>B) similarity, not necessarily congruence</p>\n<p>C) equal area only</p>\n<p>D) they are right triangles</p>\n<p>UNIT 10: Parallelograms and Triangles</p>\n<h4>19. Diagonals of a parallelogram</h4>\n<p>A) are always equal</p>\n<p>B) bisect each other</p>\n<p>C) are always perpendicular</p>\n<p>D) never intersect</p>\n<p>20. Midpoint theorem: the segment joining midpoints of two sides is:</p>\n<p>A) equal to the third side</p>\n<p>B) perpendicular to the third side</p>\n<p>C) parallel to the third side and half as long</p>\n<p>D) twice the third side</p>\n<p>UNIT 11: Line Bisectors and Angles Bisectors</p>\n<p>21. A point on the perpendicular bisector of AB is:</p>\n<p>A) closer to A than to B</p>\n<p>B) equidistant from A and B</p>\n<p>C) the midpoint of AB only</p>\n<p>D) on line AB</p>\n<p>22. Angle bisectors of a triangle meet at the:</p>\n<p>A) centroid</p>\n<p>B) orthocentre</p>\n<p>C) circumcentre</p>\n<p>D) incentre</p>\n<p>UNIT 12: Sides and Angles of a Triangle</p>\n<p>23. Which lengths can be sides of a triangle?</p>\n<h4>A) 2, 3, 6</h4>\n<h4>B) 3, 4, 5</h4>\n<h4>C) 1, 2, 3</h4>\n<h4>D) 5, 1, 1</h4>\n<p>24. In a triangle, the side opposite the greatest angle is:</p>\n<p>A) the shortest side</p>\n<p>B) the longest side</p>\n<p>C) a median</p>\n<p>D) always the base</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="5">\n<h2>Page 5</h2>\n<p>UNIT 13: Practical Geometry - Triangles</p>\n<h3>25. SAS construction uses</h3>\n<p>A) three sides</p>\n<p>B) two sides and the included angle</p>\n<p>C) two angles and no side</p>\n<p>D) hypotenuse only</p>\n<p>26. Medians of a triangle are concurrent at the:</p>\n<p>A) incentre</p>\n<p>B) circumcentre</p>\n<p>C) centroid</p>\n<p>D) orthocentre</p>\n<p>UNIT 14: Theorems Related with Area</p>\n<p>27. Area of a triangle with base 10 and height 6 is:</p>\n<h4>A) 16</h4>\n<h4>B) 30</h4>\n<h4>C) 60</h4>\n<h4>D) 120</h4>\n<p>28. Parallelograms on the same base and between the same parallels have:</p>\n<p>A) equal perimeters</p>\n<p>B) equal areas</p>\n<p>C) equal diagonals</p>\n<p>D) congruent shapes</p>\n<p>UNIT 15: Projection of a side of a Triangle</p>\n<p>29. In a right triangle with legs 3 and 4, the hypotenuse is:</p>\n<h4>A) 5</h4>\n<h4>B) 6</h4>\n<h4>C) 7</h4>\n<h4>D) 12</h4>\n<p>30. Pythagoras a^2 + b^2 = c^2 applies when the triangle is:</p>\n<p>A) any triangle</p>\n<p>B) equilateral only</p>\n<p>C) right-angled</p>\n<p>D) obtuse only</p>\n<p>UNIT 16: Introduction to Coordinate Geometry / Analytical Geometry</p>\n<h4>31. Distance between (1,2) and (4,6) is</h4>\n<h4>A) 4</h4>\n<h4>B) 5</h4>\n<h4>C) 6</h4>\n<h4>D) 7</h4>\n<h4>32. Midpoint of (2,3) and (6,7) is</h4>\n<h4>A) (4,4)</h4>\n<h4>B) (4,5)</h4>\n<h4>C) (8,10)</h4>\n<h4>D) (3,4)</h4>\n<p>ANSWER KEY</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="6">\n<h2>Page 6</h2>\n<p>1 C sqrt(2) is irrational</p>\n<p>2 A conjugate of a+ib is a-ib, so 3+5i</p>\n<h3>3 B 10^3 = 1000</h3>\n<p>4 C log 10 = 1</p>\n<p>5 B non-negative integer powers only</p>\n<p>6 C 4ab = (a+b)^2 - (a-b)^2 = 24, ab=6</p>\n<p>7 B 3+4=7 and 3*4=12</p>\n<p>8 A p(1)=0</p>\n<p>9 B common lowest powers: 6xy</p>\n<p>10 C HCF*LCM = product</p>\n<p>11 B 3x=15, x=5</p>\n<p>12 B divide by -2, reverse inequality</p>\n<p>13 B x=constant is vertical</p>\n<p>14 D (+,-) is quadrant IV</p>\n<p>15 C x=±3</p>\n<h4>16 B D=36-36=0</h4>\n<p>17 C RHS</p>\n<p>18 B AAA is similarity</p>\n<p>19 B diagonals bisect each other</p>\n<p>20 C parallel and half</p>\n<p>21 B equidistant from endpoints</p>\n<p>22 D incentre</p>\n<h4>23 B 3+4&gt;5</h4>\n<p>24 B longest side opposite greatest angle</p>\n<p>25 B two sides and included angle</p>\n<p>26 C centroid</p>\n<h4>27 B (1/2)*10*6=30</h4>\n<p>28 B equal areas</p>\n<h4>29 A 9+16=25</h4>\n<p>30 C right-angled only</p>\n<p>31 B sqrt(9+16)=5</p>\n<p>32 B averages (4,5)</p>\n<p>END OF DIAGNOSTIC</p>\n</section>', 'file_name': 'diagnostic_qa_math_ix_sindh.pdf', 'rag_thread_id': 'user_12_conv_24_1790650685_0266d7d7', 'conversation_id': 24, 'created_at': '2026-09-28T19:58:29.322852', 'updated_at': '2026-09-28T19:58:29.322862', 'is_public': True, 'has_child_version': False, 'parent_lesson_id': None, 'version': 1, 'lesson_id': 'L1790625509305', 'version_number': 1, 'parent_version_id': None, 'original_content': '# Legacy check 125803\n\n**Source document:** diagnostic_qa_math_ix_sindh.pdf\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="2">\n<h2>Page 2</h2>\n<h3>Grade 9 Mathematics - Diagnostic Assessment</h3>\n<p>Class: IX / SSC-I Sindh | Topics match the Class IX target content PDF</p>\n<p>Total: 32 Multiple Choice Questions (2 per unit)</p>\n<p>INSTRUCTIONS: Choose the best answer (A, B, C, or D).</p>\n<