T 8: Quadratic Equations</p>\n<p>15. Solve x^2 - 9 = 0.</p>\n<p>A) x=3 only</p>\n<p>B) x=-3 only</p>\n<p>C) x=3 or x=-3</p>\n<p>D) x=9 or x=-9</p>\n<p>16. Discriminant of x^2 + 6x + 9 = 0 is:</p>\n<h4>A) -36</h4>\n<h4>B) 0</h4>\n<h4>C) 9</h4>\n<h4>D) 36</h4>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="4">\n<h2>Page 4</h2>\n<p>UNIT 9: Congruent Triangles</p>\n<p>17. Which condition proves congruence of right triangles using hypotenuse and one leg?</p>\n<p>A) SSS</p>\n<p>B) ASA</p>\n<p>C) RHS</p>\n<p>D) AAA</p>\n<h4>18. AAA for two triangles shows</h4>\n<p>A) congruence always</p>\n<p>B) similarity, not necessarily congruence</p>\n<p>C) equal area only</p>\n<p>D) they are right triangles</p>\n<p>UNIT 10: Parallelograms and Triangles</p>\n<h4>19. Diagonals of a parallelogram</h4>\n<p>A) are always equal</p>\n<p>B) bisect each other</p>\n<p>C) are always perpendicular</p>\n<p>D) never intersect</p>\n<p>20. Midpoint theorem: the segment joining midpoints of two sides is:</p>\n<p>A) equal to the third side</p>\n<p>B) perpendicular to the third side</p>\n<p>C) parallel to the third side and half as long</p>\n<p>D) twice the third side</p>\n<p>UNIT 11: Line Bisectors and Angles Bisectors</p>\n<p>21. A point on the perpendicular bisector of AB is:</p>\n<p>A) closer to A than to B</p>\n<p>B) equidistant from A and B</p>\n<p>C) the midpoint of AB only</p>\n<p>D) on line AB</p>\n<p>22. Angle bisectors of a triangle meet at the:</p>\n<p>A) centroid</p>\n<p>B) orthocentre</p>\n<p>C) circumcentre</p>\n<p>D) incentre</p>\n<p>UNIT 12: Sides and Angles of a Triangle</p>\n<p>23. Which lengths can be sides of a triangle?</p>\n<h4>A) 2, 3, 6</h4>\n<h4>B) 3, 4, 5</h4>\n<h4>C) 1, 2, 3</h4>\n<h4>D) 5, 1, 1</h4>\n<p>24. In a triangle, the side opposite the greatest angle is:</p>\n<p>A) the shortest side</p>\n<p>B) the longest side</p>\n<p>C) a median</p>\n<p>D) always the base</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class="uploaded-doc-page" data-page="5">\n<h2>Page 5</h2>\n<p>UNIT 13: